Version 0.1.0

o FIX: the normal random number generator (ziggurat) used the same low bits for
  the strip index and the value and was seeded with the clock only (parallel
  workers could share streams); it now uses independent bits and the same
  seeding as the uniform generator (clock, process ID, and a counter). Uniform
  draws are strictly between 0 and 1, integer seeds are accepted, and sampling
  with replacement checks its size (shared with {psychNetwoRk})

o CHANGE: the default `fit_value` for `metric = "srmr"` in
  `add_population_error` is 0.05 (0.08 often required more error than the true
  model can absorb with proper uniquenesses, particularly for `method = "hb"`)

o CHANGE: `factor_forest` is much faster and no longer downloads its model: the
  pre-trained XGBoost model (Goretzko & Buhner, 2020) is included with the
  package (`inst/extdata/tunedxgb.json`) and loaded once per session; parallel
  analysis uses random normal reference data (as `psych::fa.parallel(fa =
  "fa")`) without computing correlations of resampled data; the comparison data
  approach (`EFA.Comp.Data`) uses an optimized implementation of the same
  algorithm (adapted from the Non-hierarchical-PRISM project)

o FIX: `factor_forest` computed the empirical Kaiser criterion feature
  differently from the model's training code (`which(eigenvalues <=
  reference)[1] - 1`)

o CHANGE (BREAKING): `factor_forest` requires data (correlation matrices are no
  longer accepted because the comparison data feature is computed from the data;
  previously it was silently computed on the correlation matrix); `sample_size`
  is deprecated and ignored. `estimate_dimensions` skips Factor Forest with a
  warning for correlation matrix input

o INTERNAL: removed the {mlr}, {BBmisc}, and {googledrive} dependencies (only
  used to load and predict with the downloaded Factor Forest model)

o CHANGE: `add_population_error` optimization is 6-35x faster with the same
  accuracy (achieved fit within 1e-6 of the target, typically ~1e-10): Fisher
  scoring in the true-model refit no longer iterates on rounding error, refits
  are warm-started (and confirmed from the true parameters), the target is found
  by searching upward from no error (smallest error that reaches the target)
  instead of a 20-point grid, and the Cudeck-Browne error is scaled in closed
  form

o CHANGE: `add_population_error` checks that the true model is identified (e.g.,
  `leave_cross_loadings = TRUE` with cross-loadings on every variable is
  rejected because its degrees of freedom are not defined), warns about Heywood
  cases, accepts `add_method_factors` output (method factors are part of the
  true model), and derives correlated residuals from the population correlation
  matrix so that repeated `add_local_dependence` calls are kept

o NEW: `print`/`summary` methods describe the original simulated data and then
  each modification in order (cross-loadings, local dependence, method factors,
  wording effects, population error, Zipf's); `estimate_dimensions` output
  prints a table of dimensions; `EKC`, `NEST`, `factor_forest`, `ESEM`, and
  `obtain_zipfs_parameters` outputs have classes (`lf_ekc`, `lf_nest`, `lf_ff`,
  `lf_esem`, `lf_zipfs_parameters`) with `print`/`summary` methods (returned
  elements are unchanged)

o CHANGE: `simulate_hierarchical_factors` output has class `c("lf_simulate",
  "lf_hierarchical")`; `add_*` functions reject hierarchical input with an
  informative error

o CHANGE: `add_cross_loadings`, `add_method_factors`, and `add_wording_effects`
  re-simulate data from the factor model, so they now reject input with local
  dependence (`add_local_dependence` output) instead of silently dropping the
  correlated residuals; apply local dependence after these functions (and
  population error last)

o FIX: `add_method_factors` applied per-variable skew to the wrong variables
  with mixed categorical/continuous data and did not return
  `population_correlation`

o FIX: `add_local_dependence` could pair a variable with itself or repeat a pair
  in reverse order (`allow_multiple = TRUE`), merged only two of several
  connected residual chains when aligning skew, and could misalign per-factor
  `add_residuals` when the number of pairs was reduced

o FIX: `add_wording_effects` (`method = "difficulty"`) indexed past the loadings
  when all loadings were negative

o FIX: `NEST` reported uniqueness rows as loadings when fewer factors than
  requested were estimated, could fail when the loop never stopped, and warned
  about convergence when it had converged on the last iteration

o FIX: `estimate_dimensions` returned an unnamed parallel analysis result on
  error, could pick up `pa_nfact`/`pa_ncomp` from the global environment,
  errored when Factor Forest returned `NA`, and did not merge user `FSPE_args`
  with the defaults

o FIX: `ESEM` (`wording_factor = "CTCM1_each"`) reused the previous factor's
  syntax for factors without target-sign variables, and could pick up
  `parameters` from the global environment

o FIX: `factor_forest` passed all of `PA_correlation`'s choices to
  `psych::fa.parallel` (now `match.arg`);
  `simulate_factors`/`simulate_hierarchical_factors` gave "object
  'total_variables' not found" for a `variables` of the wrong length;
  `simulate_hierarchical_factors` now redraws
  `off_disturbances_range`/`lower_correlations_range` on every retry

o INTERNAL: shared helpers in `helpers-simulation.R` (`generate_lf_data`,
  `check_lf_object`, `variable_names`, `resolve_proportion`,
  `flip_dominant_loadings`, `dominant_signs`, `draw_correlation_matrix`,
  `snap_skew_range`) replace code that was duplicated across the simulation
  functions

o INTERNAL: removed {testthat} (validation is run outside of the package)

o CHANGE (BREAKING): `add_population_error` now uses the population error
  methods of {psychNetwoRk}, adapted to factor models. The error is generated
  with `method = "cb"` (Cudeck & Browne, 1992), `"tkl"` (Tucker, Koopman, &
  Linn, 1969), `"wb"` (Wu & Browne, 2015), or `"hb"` (Hu & Bentler, 1998;
  omitted cross-loadings) and optimized so that a fit index (`metric` = CFI,
  TLI, RMSEA, or SRMR) of the true factor model fit to the population
  correlation matrix with error (maximum likelihood, evaluated at the size of
  the human population) equals `fit_value`. The arguments `cfa_method`, `fit`,
  `misfit`, `error_method`, and `convergence_iterations` were replaced by
  `method`, `metric`, `fit_value`, `max_iterations`, and `control`. The
  `population_error` element of the output now contains `method`, `metric`,
  `fit_value`, `achieved`, `fit`, `delta`, `error`, `max_error`, `R_difference`,
  `loadings`, `factor_correlations`, and `iterations`

o FIX: the previous population error implementation compared SRMR against the
  target regardless of `fit` (e.g., CFI = 0.95 was checked against an SRMR of
  0.95). The achieved fit now equals the requested fit to within 1e-6 (when
  reachable), is reported in `population_error$achieved`, and is verified
  against {lavaan}

o FIX: `add_population_error` no longer drops correlated residuals added by
  `add_local_dependence` from the true model, and applies the skew of continuous
  variables and per-variable categorical skew like `simulate_factors`

o CHANGE: `print` and `summary` methods work with the output of
  `add_population_error` and report the population error

o INTERNAL: removed the {bifactor}-derived `cudeck`, `yuan`, and `CFA` functions
  (and their helpers) and the {bifactor} citation message; population error
  functions now live in `error_optimizations.R`

o NEW: `seed` argument for reproducible results in `simulate_factors`,
  `simulate_hierarchical_factors`, `add_cross_loadings`, `add_local_dependence`,
  `add_method_factors`, `add_wording_effects`, `add_population_error`,
  `data_to_zipfs`, `NEST`, `factor_forest`, and `estimate_dimensions`; the same
  seed gives the same results and a seeded call does not change the R session's
  random number stream (previously results could not be reproduced because the C
  random number generators were seeded from the clock)

o INTERNAL: all random numbers are generated with the package's C random number
  generators (xoshiro256++ and ziggurat), including data generation (previously
  `mvtnorm::rmvnorm`), the comparison data approach in `factor_forest`, and
  `data_to_zipfs` (previously R's `rnorm`/`sample`); {mvtnorm} is no longer a
  dependency

o FIX (behavior change): `simulate_hierarchical_factors` added a supplied
  `off_disturbances`/`off_disturbances_range` value r to the lower-order factor
  covariances directly, so the disturbance correlation was r / (1 - h^2) rather
  than r; the disturbance covariances are now r * sqrt((1 - h^2_i)(1 - h^2_j)),
  so `off_disturbances` are the disturbance correlations (as documented) and the
  lower-order factors have unit variance without rescaling.
  `parameters$higher$disturbances` is the disturbance covariance matrix (its
  diagonal holds the disturbance variances); `print`/`summary` report
  disturbance correlations

o CHANGE: `simulate_factors` accepts per-variable `loadings` (a vector as long
  as the total number of variables), as documented; other lengths now give an
  error instead of being silently truncated (also for
  `lower_loadings`/`higher_loadings` in `simulate_hierarchical_factors`)

o FIX: `skew_range` draws one skew per variable (previously, if the sequence of
  skews in the range had as many values as there are variables, they were
  applied in order rather than sampled)

o FIX: `range_error` (input checks) rounded inputs to three decimals before
  comparing, so values slightly outside the allowed range (e.g., 1.0004 for a
  correlation) were accepted

o INTERNAL: `factor_forest` checks that its features match the order the model
  was trained on

Version 0.0.9

o CHANGE: skew range supported by `categorize`, `simulate_factors`, and
  `simulate_hierarchical_factors` expanded from -2/2 to -10/10 (in increments of
  0.05); `skew_tables` regenerated over the new range

o INTERNAL: `skew_generator` rewritten to compute skewness analytically from
  category counts instead of materializing a `sample_size`-length vector and
  calling `psych::skew()` on it (mathematically equivalent, since the
  moment-skewness formula only depends on counts per value); it also now
  searches adaptively over `reduction_factor` and `sample_size` when the
  defaults can't reach a target skew directly, closing gaps (e.g., skew near
  zero for `categories >= 4`) that were previously unreachable regardless of
  tolerance. Together, this cut full skew-table generation time from tens of
  minutes to about a second

o FIX: `skew_generator`'s bisection search had a typo (`limitinf` instead of
  `limitsinf`) that left the lower search bound frozen at zero, which combined
  with `skew(proportion)` not being monotonic across its full domain, could
  prevent convergence even for reachable targets

Version 0.0.8

o NEW: `simulate_hierarchical_factors` simulates data from a hierarchical
  (second-order) factor model, where variables load onto lower-order factors and
  lower-order factors load onto higher-order factors; the lower-order factors'
  residual (disturbance) structure can be set directly via
  `off_disturbances`/`off_disturbances_range` (only their *off-diagonal*,
  correlated-disturbance elements are settable — the diagonal is always rescaled
  to unit variance, since any unexplained variance is normalized away
  regardless) or left to be implied instead by supplying a target
  `lower_correlations`/`lower_correlations_range` matrix

o NEW: `print`/`summary` methods for
  `simulate_factors`/`simulate_hierarchical_factors` output, reporting loadings,
  correlations, disturbances, categories, and skew

o FIX: `simulate_factors` could loop indefinitely when supplied
  `loadings`/`correlations` were jointly inadmissible; now errors after 1000
  attempts with an informative message

o INTERNAL: consolidated `@import`/`@importFrom` roxygen tags into a single
  package-level block in `latentFactoR.R`

o INTERNAL: cleaned up and optimized code in `simulate_factors`

o INTERNAL: random number generation moved to C

o INTERNAL: cleaned up and optimized code in `add_cross_loadings`,
  `add_local_dependence`, `add_method_factors`, `add_population_error`, and
  `add_wording_effects` to match `simulate_factors` (uses C-based random number
  generation)

o FIX: `add_cross_loadings` could error with "missing value where TRUE/FALSE
  needed" when a factor was randomly assigned zero cross-loadings (e.g., via
  `proportion_cross_loadings_range`)

o FIX: `proportion_negative_range` in `add_method_factors` was documented but
  not implemented; it now works as documented

o FIX: documentation in `simulate_factors` was missing the `continuous_data`
  return value and the `cross_loadings`/`categorical_limit` parameters list, and
  had the loadings matrix dimensions reversed

o FIX: `simulate_factors`/`simulate_hierarchical_factors` misassigned
  per-variable `skew` values to the wrong columns whenever a full-length `skew`
  vector was supplied on a model mixing categorical and continuous variables

o FIX: `simulate_factors`/`simulate_hierarchical_factors` ignored
  `loadings_range`/`correlations_range` (and the hierarchical equivalents) after
  the first retry attempt, so the admissibility retry loop could never actually
  resample those parameters

o FIX: `add_cross_loadings` could error with "missing value where TRUE/FALSE
  needed" when `magnitude_cross_loadings` was supplied with length equal to
  `factors` (a documented, valid length)

o FIX: `add_cross_loadings` now errors informatively (instead of crashing) when
  called on data with only one factor, since cross-loadings require at least two
  factors

o FIX: `add_local_dependence` could error with "NAs are not allowed in
  subscripted assignments" when composed after `add_cross_loadings` had already
  consumed most of a factor's available variables

o FIX: `add_cross_loadings`, `add_local_dependence`, `add_method_factors`, and
  `add_wording_effects` incorrectly indexed `parameters$variables` when
  converting a raw-count `_range` argument (e.g.
  `proportion_cross_loadings_range`) into a proportion, which could silently
  misconvert the range or crash outright for a single-factor model

o FIX: `add_local_dependence`/`add_method_factors` admissibility retry loops had
  no maximum-iteration guard and could loop indefinitely

o FIX: `add_population_error` reused the first random population-error draw on
  every retry iteration instead of resampling, making `convergence_iterations`
  ineffective

o FIX: `add_population_error` applied its CFA sign-indeterminacy correction
  globally across the whole loadings matrix instead of per-factor, which could
  corrupt an otherwise correctly-signed factor's loadings in multi-factor models

o FIX: `ESEM` did not resolve its `estimator` argument via `match.arg`, so the
  documented default (omitting `estimator`) failed; `estimator` now defaults to
  `"MLR"`

o FIX: `ESEM` built lavaan variable names with a hardcoded zero-padding width
  that mismatched the actual data column names for models with fewer than 10 or
  100+ total variables

o FIX: `NEST`/`factor_forest`/`estimate_dimensions` used base `isSymmetric()`
  (which requires matching row and column names) to detect correlation-matrix
  input; an unnamed but valid correlation matrix was misclassified as raw data,
  silently discarding a user-supplied `sample_size`

o FIX: `NEST` could error with a non-conformable matrix multiplication when its
  internal rank-reduction step truncated below the requested number of factors

o FIX: `EKC` returned `NA` instead of the maximum number of dimensions when
  every eigenvalue exceeded its reference value

o FIX: `obtain_zipfs_parameters` could not recover `alpha < 1` due to a
  hardcoded search floor, despite `alpha < 1` being a documented, valid input to
  `data_to_zipfs`

o FIX: `data_to_zipfs`'s zero-sum-column repair silently failed when the
  most-correlated donor column was itself all-zero

o FIX: `factor_forest`'s downloaded model cache path used a Windows-style
  backslash separator, breaking cache detection (forcing re-download every call)
  on Linux/macOS

o FIX: `factor_forest`'s eigenvalue-padding step could run backward and
  overwrite real eigenvalues for datasets with 80 or more variables

o FIX: `simulate_factors` enforced a minimum of 2 variables per factor in code
  while documenting (and enforcing via `variables_range`) a minimum of 3

o INTERNAL: removed dead/unreachable code (`error.report`, a duplicated
  `digits()` definition, a non-functional `.onload` typo) and added
  `URL`/`BugReports` to `DESCRIPTION`; `inst/CITATION` now derives its
  version/year from `DESCRIPTION` instead of a hardcoded string

Version 0.0.7

o FIX: error check for positive definite in `add_population_error`

o FIX: correction when adding negative skew for continuous data (internal
  function: `skew_continuous`)

o CHANGE: better ML population error in `add_population_error` (update from
  {bifactor})

o CHANGE: some optimizations to `add_population_error`

o CHANGE: some optimiztaions to `NEST`

Version 0.0.6

o CHANGE: `add_wording_effects` methods moved from `utils-latentFactoR` to
  `add_wording_effects-helpers` to facilitate ease of finding code (no
  observable changes to the user)

o FIX: categories greater than 7 were not previously allowed (they are now)

o FIX: correlations in `EKC` were not used appropriately and led to an error

o CHANGE: switched on "Byte-Compile" (byte-compiles on our end and not when the
  user installs)

Version 0.0.5

o FIX: bug in skew when only providing 1 value

o FIX: further correction to `EKC` (uses `cumsum(eigenvalues)` rather than
  `sum(eigenvalues)`)

o NEW: `add_wording_effects` will add wording effects such as acquiescence,
  difficulty, random careless, straight line, or some combination of the four to
  a simulated factor model

o NEW: `ESEM` to perform Exploratory Structural Equation Modeling using {lavaan}
  (allows wording effects to be estimated)

o FIX: `factor_forest` uses raw data in `psych::fa.parallel` rather than
  correlation matrix

o NEW: internal functions for computing effect sizes across conditions are
  included (see `simulation_helpers.R`)

o NEW: skew in `add_local_dependence` is guaranteed to be same direction for
  locally dependent variables

Version 0.0.4

o FIX: correction to `EKC` (used `factor_forest`'s version of EKC which used
  reference values rather than eigenvalues); `EKC` uses eigenvalues whereas
  `factor_forest` uses reference (which was what the random forest model was
  trained on)

o FIX: cross-loadings with population error are screened for communalities >=
  0.80; communalities near 0.90 prior to population error would often get stuck
  and not converge

o NEW: `skew` argument for continuous data

Version 0.0.3

o NEW: `add_population_error` will add population error, using {bifactor}, to a
  simulated factor model

o NEW: `data_to_zipfs` to transform data to Zipf's distribution from
  `simulate_factors`

o NEW: `obtain_zipfs_parameters` to obtain a dataset's best fitting Zipf's
  distribution parameters

o NEW: `NEST` Next Eigenvalue Sufficiency Test to estimate dimensions

o NEW: `estimate_dimensions` provides a single function to estimate dimensions
  using state-of-the-art methods: Exploratory Graph Analysis (EGA), Exploratory
  Factor Analysis with out-of-sample prediction (FSPE), Next Eigenvalue
  Sufficiency Test (NEST), parallel analysis (PA), and Factor Forest

Version 0.0.2

o CHANGE: skews for categories now include 6 categories

o CHANGE: available skew increments are now 0.05 (were 0.50 previously)

o NEW: `add_local_dependence` will add local dependence between variables from a
  simulated factor model

Version 0.0.1

o NEW: initial release
